  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about which design choices (modality, encoder, profile, aggregation, fusion) matter. The most fundamental uncertainty is what a simple baseline achieves, without which no subsequent comparison is interpretable. 2) What this tests: config_001 (RNA-only, single-profile, PCA-2000) is the simplest possible configuration—no deep encoder, no aggregation, no fusion. It establishes the lower-bound performance of the RNA modality with a linear representation and a single profile. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Under a limited budget, the first run should be fast, unambiguous, and maximally informative as a reference. A PCA baseline is cheap to train, removes confounds from encoder choice and fusion strategy, and provides a concrete number against which every later experiment (deeper RNA encoders, image-only, multimodal, multiprofile, various aggregations/fusions) can be directly compared. Starting here avoids overcommitting to any complex design decision before we know what the data can support."
